WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_k8s_151'), 'Five api pods without any `resources:` ran on one node while the other node was nearly empty:

```text
$ kubectl get pods -o wide -n rice-rocket
NAME                   READY   STATUS    NODE
api-5c9d7f6b8-2xkqz    1/1     Running   pool-a-1
api-5c9d7f6b8-7hd2m    1/1     Running   pool-a-1
api-5c9d7f6b8-9pq4r    1/1     Running   pool-a-1
api-5c9d7f6b8-kd8w1    1/1     Running   pool-a-1
api-5c9d7f6b8-x7v3n    1/1     Running   pool-a-1
$ kubectl top nodes
NAME       CPU(cores)   CPU%   MEMORY(bytes)   MEMORY%
pool-a-1   1810m        93%    3021Mi          89%
pool-a-2   95m          4%     610Mi           18%
```

Statement: "The scheduler will move some of these pods to pool-a-2 on its own once it sees pool-a-1 at 93% CPU."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The scheduler places a pod once, when it is created, by adding up the pods'' **requests** against each node''s allocatable; it never reads live usage and never moves a running pod. Pods with no requests count as zero, so all five fit on pool-a-1. They stay there until they are deleted (or evicted). Setting requests (`cpu: 250m`, `memory: 256Mi`) makes the scheduler spread new pods; a rollout (`kubectl rollout restart deployment/api`) then places them again. The misconception is that Kubernetes balances running load by itself.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_k8s_151') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
