WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_k8s_151'), 'During the report export the api restarted three times:

```text
$ kubectl get pod api-5c9d7f6b8-7hd2m -o jsonpath=''{.status.containerStatuses[0].lastState}''
{"terminated":{"exitCode":137,"reason":"OOMKilled","startedAt":"2026-11-03T02:10:41Z","finishedAt":"2026-11-03T02:12:07Z"}}
$ kubectl get pod api-5c9d7f6b8-7hd2m -o jsonpath=''{.spec.containers[0].resources}''
{"limits":{"memory":"768Mi"},"requests":{"cpu":"700m","memory":"512Mi"}}
```

Statement: "The container went over its own 768Mi memory limit and the kernel killed it (137 = SIGKILL); the fix is a higher limit or a report export that streams instead of holding every row in memory."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. Reason `OOMKilled` with exit code 137 (128 + 9, SIGKILL) means the cgroup of this container hit its memory limit and the kernel''s OOM killer ended the process; the kubelet then restarted it. It is not a node problem (that would show as an eviction, `reason: Evicted`). Either the limit is too low for the real peak, or the code holds too much (here: building the whole export in memory). The misconception is to look for an app bug in the logs: an OOM-killed process usually logs nothing at all.', 3, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_k8s_151') AND order_index = 3)
    RETURNING id
)
