WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_k8s_181'), 'After a Secret was recreated by hand, a new rollout is stuck:

```text
$ kubectl get pods -n rice-rocket -l app=api
NAME                  READY   STATUS                       RESTARTS   AGE
api-6d8f7c9b5-zt4qw   0/1     CreateContainerConfigError   0          2m
$ kubectl describe pod api-6d8f7c9b5-zt4qw -n rice-rocket | tail -2
  Warning  Failed  12s (x9 over 2m)  kubelet  Error: couldn''t find key db_password in Secret rice-rocket/api-secrets
$ kubectl get secret api-secrets -n rice-rocket -o jsonpath=''{.data}'' | jq ''keys''
["db-password", "jwt-secret"]
```

Statement: "The container never started: the Deployment asks for the key `db_password` but the recreated Secret has `db-password`. Deleting the pod or restarting the rollout will not help until the key name matches."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. `CreateContainerConfigError` happens before the process starts, while the kubelet builds the container''s environment from `secretKeyRef`. There is no log and the restart count stays 0. A new pod reads the same Secret and fails the same way. Fix either the Secret (recreate it with the key `db_password`) or the Deployment''s `key:`; the waiting pod then starts on the next retry. The misconception is that every not-ready pod is fixed by a restart.', 3, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_k8s_181') AND order_index = 3)
    RETURNING id
)
